  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nCurrently near zonal flow exist over the Northern Plains with a\nslight southwest tilt over Montana. A trough and upper low digs\nthrough Washington state at this time. At the surface a low \nsits in eastern Wyoming with an inverted trough through eastern \nMontana with showers and thunderstorms around the low.\n\nToday we are in another severe risk, this time just a level 1\nacross the southern half of the state. Global models have 1000\nto 2000 J/kg of MUCAPE with 45 to 50kts of shear. The actual 18z\nsounding from Bismarck shows 1900 J/kg of CAPE from the surface\nwith very little CIN and 53 kts of shear. So far very light rain\nfrom the low pressure is in the southwest with thunderstorms in\nsoutheast Montana. A few failing storms are trying in south \ncentral North Dakota. The forcing and indicator for where the \nsevere storms will be is the right entrance region of the jet \nstreak. Currently that area is over southeastern Montana, \nentering into southwest North Dakota. Looking at radar new \nstorms are quickly growing in southeast Montana. That jet streak\nis forecast to move east with the right entrance region staying\nalong the South Dakota border. After 00z, models want to pull \nmost of the CAPE out of the state and into eastern South Dakota \nas the surface low moves east. After this evening showers and \nmaybe a thunderstorm are possible through the night.\n\nThe rest of this week the flow will turn southwest and the\ntrough continuing to dig into the west CONUS. This, in turn, \nwill produce daily chances of showers and thunderstorms through \nFriday night, with the highest chances across the west everyday.\nTemperatures will continue to remain at or below normal in the \n70s and lower 80s through Saturday. Slight northwest flow \nreturns this weekend with a slight ridge to start next week. \nTherefore normal temperatures in the 80s will return.",
